From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] WM Jetpack: show snapshots of the containers while dragging
 the divider

While the divider is being dragged, DividerPresenter covers both
containers with solid veils and resizes the containers only when the
drag ends, so that the apps do not lay out again on every move. The
veil color is the window background of the top activity if it is a
color, or black and gray. Many apps paint their own background over a
window background of another color; in the dark theme of one such app
the window background is #000000 under a page drawn in #191B1F, and
both sides of the split turned black while dragging.

When dragging starts, capture the window of the top activity of each
container, with its child surfaces such as video, and show the capture
on the veil where it was taken. The veil crops it to the container's
new bounds, so the divider slides over the content instead of hiding
it. The part of a growing container that the capture does not cover is
filled with the most common opaque color of a 4px strip of the window
next to the divider, unless the app configured a veil color. Without
READ_FRAME_BUFFER, SurfaceFlinger lets an app capture only the layers
of its own uid, which these windows are. The veil shows a solid color
as before when the container has activities of another process (which
cannot be captured here, and one may be on top), when the window does
not cover exactly the current bounds of the container (letterboxed or
scaled for size compatibility), when it has secure content (captured
black), or when a capture fails. The snapshots are removed together with the veils when
the drag ends; their buffers are closed once the transaction holds
them.

The captures (a window and a strip on each side) run synchronously
when dragging starts; with two 1280x1600 windows they took about 20 ms
in total on this device. Reading the strip back from the hardware
bitmap is exempted from StrictMode's slow-call check.

